Approved behavior:
37:| BEH-011 | Integrated `delegate_task.recipient_name` already uses the same `/...` and `./...` resolver as messaging, then applies current-Team direct-target task eligibility and configured coordinator entry. | Rename only the public selector to `recipient_address`, resolve through the rooted TeamRun tree, and preserve the same operation-owned eligibility/lifecycle mapping. | Task eligibility remains limited to direct physical Agents/child Teams of the caller's immediate Team; task creation, task-Agent/task-AgentTeam materialization, `submit_task_result`, `review_task_result`, settlement, and exact task AgentRun communication retain task-protocol ownership. | R-023, R-027; AC-018, AC-022 |
189:- **R-027 — Hierarchical task recipient selector:** `delegate_task` shall accept one required `recipient_address` string using exactly the R-007 grammar and shall remove the public `{target:{kind,name}}` selector and any `recipient_name` alias. Resolution shall infer Agent versus AgentTeam from the same canonical recipient returned for `send_message_to`. After shared resolution, existing task eligibility remains separately enforced: the recipient must be a direct physical Agent or direct child AgentTeam of the caller's immediate Team, and the caller Agent remains ineligible. An eligible Agent starts task-Agent execution; an eligible AgentTeam starts task-AgentTeam execution through its configured coordinator. A resolved non-direct recipient fails with `TASK_DELEGATION_TARGET_NOT_ELIGIBLE`, without name fallback, caller-supplied kind, or a separate task roster authority.
